ask(c,'Which condition is essential to the lecture’s finite-convergence claim for the ordinary perceptron algorithm?', ['A finite training set that is linearly separable, with mistaken examples revisited under the update rule','Every feature value must be strictly positive','The model must be a single unit with sigmoid output instead of a threshold','The learning rate must grow without bound after each mistake'],['Separability and a proper mistake-driven sampling procedure support convergence; arbitrary nonseparable labels do not.','Feature signs do not determine separability.','The shown algorithm uses a hard decision, not sigmoid-gradient training.','An unbounded schedule is not the stated condition.'],assumptions=['Finite data; standard positive fixed update scale; fair revisiting of mistakes.'],pages=[1032,1033],locator='Linear-separability assumption and convergence loop')
c=concept(1033,'Complete perceptron update and dot-product reasoning','Both error branches, threshold equality, augmented bias, score increase versus one-step success.')
upd=fm('w_new=w+x for a positive error; w_new=w-x for a negative error',{'w':'current augmented weight vector','x':'augmented input vector including constant1 if bias is trained','w_new':'updated augmented weights'},{'all':'dimensionless'},['Unit update scale; label1 fires at w·x>=0.'])
ask(c,'Read the actual pseudocode inset, including the branch omitted from the prose explanation. Which updates are specified?', ['Add x for a misclassified positive; subtract x for a misclassified negative','Add x for both kinds of mistake','Subtract x for both kinds of mistake','Update positive mistakes only and ignore every negative mistake'],['Both branches are visible in the inset.','Negative mistakes use subtraction.','Positive mistakes use addition.','That would overlook the inset’s second branch.'],kind='algorithm_trace',steps=['Check the example label and current score.','For positive with score<0 add x; for negative with score>=0 subtract x.'],formula=[upd],citekind='code',locator='Algorithm1 inset, both if branches')
ask(c,'A negative-labeled example has score exactly0. Under the shown convention, which branch applies?', ['It is a mistake and w becomes w-x','It is correct and weights stay unchanged','It is a positive mistake and w becomes w+x','The algorithm has no defined case at equality'],['Score0 predicts1, so a true negative triggers the >=0 subtraction branch.','That would require predicting0 at equality.','The label is negative, not positive.','The inset explicitly covers >=0.'],kind='algorithm_trace',steps=['At score0 the predicted label is1.','Compare with target0, then subtract x.'],formula=[upd],citekind='code',locator='Negative branch condition')
ask(c,'Use augmented x=(1,2,-1), w=(0,-1,0) and target1. What are the unit-step updated weights?', ['(1,1,-1)','(-1,-3,1)','(0,1,-1)','(1,-1,0)'],['The score is-2, so add x componentwise to get(1,1,-1).','This subtracts instead of adding.','This fails to update the augmented bias coordinate.','This updates only the bias.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Compute w·x=-2<0 for target1.','Add(1,2,-1) to(0,-1,0).'],formula=[upd],assumptions=['First coordinate is constant bias input1.'],citekind='code',locator='Positive error branch')
ask(c,'The prose says adding x will yield a positive dot product. For w=(-10,0) and x=(1,0), what actually happens after one positive-error update?', ['The score rises from-10 to-9 but remains incorrect','The score becomes+1 immediately','The score stays-10 because the example was already positive-labeled','The score becomes-11 because addition reverses the input'],['Adding x raises the score by||x||²=1, not enough to cross0.','This ignores the old score.','Positive label does not mean current prediction is correct.','That would be a subtractive update.'],kind='numerical',steps=['Old score=-10.','Updated w=(-9,0), so new score=-9.'],formula=[upd,fm('(w+x)·x=w·x+||x||²',{'w':'weight vector','x':'nonzero input vector'},{'all':'dimensionless'})],assumptions=['No bias coordinate in this explicitly supplied two-dimensional example.'],conflict=(1033,'One positive-error update is said to yield a positive dot product.','Exact counterexample shows score improvement without immediate sign correction.'),citekind='code',locator='Positive update and accompanying geometric prose')
ask(c,'For nonzero w and x, which statement about w·x=||w||||x||cos(alpha) is valid?', ['A negative dot product means an obtuse angle; adding x increases the dot product by||x||²','A negative dot product means an acute angle; adding x leaves the score unchanged','A zero dot product means the vectors are parallel','The angle formula remains defined when w is the zero vector without any convention'],['Positive norms preserve the sign of the cosine; the update identity follows by distributivity.','Both the angle interpretation and score-change claim are wrong.','Zero dot product of nonzero vectors means perpendicular, not parallel.','A zero vector has no uniquely defined direction or angle.'],formula=[fm('w·x=||w||||x||cos(alpha)',{'w':'nonzero weight vector','x':'nonzero input vector','alpha':'angle between them'},{'w,x':'dimensionless','alpha':'radians'},['Both vectors nonzero.'])],locator='Dot-product and angle explanation')
ask(c,'Why is “increase the weight by adding the input” not a componentwise guarantee?', ['Negative input components decrease the corresponding weight components even though the positive-example score increases','Adding a vector always increases every component','Weights are scalars even when the input is a vector','The update changes only the angle, never the coordinates'],['For each coordinate w_i becomes w_i+x_i, whose change has the sign of x_i.','This fails whenever an input component is negative.','The model has a vector of feature coefficients.','Vector addition changes coordinates.'],formula=[upd],locator='Add-input prose',conflict=(1033,'Adding x is described as increasing the current weight.','Distinguish vector addition from componentwise numerical increase; score improvement is the valid invariant.'))
issue(1033,'single-update','The prose claims adding a misclassified positive input yields a positive dot product and increases weight.','Both algorithm branches are present. Test score increment||x||², not guaranteed one-step correction or componentwise increases; include bias augmentation explicitly.')
