c=concept(1044,'Backpropagation versus optimization','Chain-rule gradients identify sensitivities; an optimizer applies updates; neither guarantees a global minimum.')
ask(c,'Which pair correctly separates backpropagation from gradient descent?', ['Backpropagation computes gradients; gradient descent uses them to update parameters','Backpropagation chooses labels; gradient descent computes raw sensor measurements','Backpropagation updates by adding random noise; gradient descent removes the hidden layers','Backpropagation is only the forward prediction; gradient descent is only evaluating the loss'],['Gradient computation and the chosen parameter-update rule are distinct operations.','Labels and measurements are data, not these algorithms’ defining outputs.','Neither operation is defined by those changes.','This exchanges both operations with earlier stages.'],kind='match',locator='Gradient calculation, backward propagation and descent bullets')
ask(c,'A parameter gradient is positive and the learning rate is positive. Which local descent update follows?', ['Decrease that parameter by learning rate times gradient','Increase that parameter by learning rate times gradient','Set the parameter equal to the gradient','Leave the parameter unchanged because the gradient is not negative'],['Descent subtracts the positive gradient contribution.','That is ascent for this coordinate.','An update modifies the old parameter rather than replacing it with a derivative.','Nonzero positive gradients also generate updates.'],locator='Opposite direction of gradient')
