ask(c,'Does setting n=1 in MSE=(1/n)sum(error²) yield0.5*error²?', ['No; it yields error², and the factor0.5 is a separate rescaling','Yes; averaging a single example always divides by2','Yes; the squared exponent automatically becomes the denominator','No; it yields absolute error instead'],['The normalization changes deliberately; it does not follow from n1.','A one-item average equals that item.','Exponent and averaging factor are unrelated operations.','Setting n1 leaves the square intact.'],formula=[mse,half],conflict=(1045,'Single-sample MSE is said to become half-squared error.','Substitute n1 explicitly and identify the extra positive scaling factor.'),locator='MSE to single-sample loss transition')
ask(c,'If a loss is multiplied by0.5, which statement is correct?', ['Its minimizers are unchanged, but its gradients halve and the same learning rate yields half the update','Its minimizers generally double','Its gradients stay unchanged because only the objective value changes','It becomes differentiable even if the original loss was not'],['Positive rescaling preserves the argmin and scales derivatives by the same factor.','Positive scaling does not move minimizers.','Differentiation is linear in constant factors.','A nonzero constant rescaling does not repair nondifferentiability.'],formula=[fm('L_new=0.5*L_old; grad(L_new)=0.5*grad(L_old)',{'L_old':'original differentiable loss','L_new':'rescaled loss','grad':'gradient with respect to the same parameter vector'},{'all':'normalized dimensionless coordinates'},['Positive constant scaling; same parameterization.'])],locator='Half factor for gradient calculation')
